WEBVTT

00:00:00.252 --> 00:00:03.829
Mia, will our magnet attract
every metal on this tray?

00:00:04.653 --> 00:00:06.615
I was going to say yes, but

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:09.144
copper is already
preparing to embarrass me.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.417
Electrical conduction and strong
magnetic attraction are not the same

00:00:13.418 --> 00:00:14.023
property.

00:00:14.815 --> 00:00:15.664
Exactly.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:21.288
Iron, nickel, cobalt, and many steels are
strongly magnetic in the everyday sense.

00:00:21.624 --> 00:00:26.237
Not every steel is strongly attracted,
and materials described as non-magnetic

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:28.654
can still have weak magnetic responses.

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:32.819
So for this classroom test,
copper and aluminium are not

00:00:32.828 --> 00:00:34.381
strongly attracted like iron.

00:00:34.887 --> 00:00:37.284
I should not write that
all metals are magnetic.

00:00:37.866 --> 00:00:40.481
Now, bring two north
poles near each other.

00:00:40.868 --> 00:00:41.662
What happens?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.534
Like poles repel, unlike poles attract.

00:00:45.928 --> 00:00:50.574
North and south pull together,
while north and north push apart.

00:00:51.346 --> 00:00:51.893
Correct.

00:00:52.282 --> 00:00:56.288
Attraction alone does not prove that
an object is a permanent magnet.

00:00:56.628 --> 00:00:59.298
An iron object can be
attracted by either pole.

00:00:59.652 --> 00:01:03.443
Repulsion between suitable ends is
stronger evidence of two magnets.

00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:06.454
What if we cut a bar magnet in half?

00:01:06.846 --> 00:01:09.667
Does one piece keep north
and the other keep south?

00:01:10.423 --> 00:01:10.789
No.

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:15.881
Cutting an ordinary bar magnet gives
smaller magnets, each with both poles.

00:01:16.264 --> 00:01:19.672
We do not isolate a north pole
by snapping off the south end.

00:01:20.125 --> 00:01:23.431
This is a diagram, not an
instruction to break magnets

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:27.708
A magnetic field describes the magnetic
influence in the surrounding region.

00:01:28.351 --> 00:01:30.743
How do we show its direction
around a bar magnet?

00:01:31.514 --> 00:01:35.362
Outside the magnet, field line
arrows go from north to south.

00:01:35.802 --> 00:01:39.484
A small compass's north-seeking
end points along the local field.

00:01:40.252 --> 00:01:40.626
Good.

00:01:41.036 --> 00:01:45.028
Lines drawn closer together indicate
a stronger field in our diagram.

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:48.578
They are a representation,
not physical strings.

00:01:49.105 --> 00:01:53.154
They do not cross because the field has
one direction at each ordinary point.

00:01:53.944 --> 00:01:55.761
Iron filings can show a pattern,

00:01:56.047 --> 00:01:56.717
but a plotting

00:01:56.717 --> 00:01:58.556
compass also gives the direction.

00:01:59.172 --> 00:02:02.313
Filings alone do not label
which way the arrows point.

00:02:03.032 --> 00:02:03.875
Exactly.

00:02:04.201 --> 00:02:07.327
Use supervised demonstrations
with contained filings.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.295
A compass also responds to nearby magnets
and current-carrying wires, so it need

00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:14.494
not always point towards geographic north.

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:19.277
The Earth's field is roughly like a
giant bar magnet's, though there is not

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.515
actually a bar magnet buried inside it.

00:02:22.338 --> 00:02:22.872
Correct.

00:02:23.264 --> 00:02:27.377
Motion of conducting material in Earth's
outer core helps generate the field.

00:02:27.659 --> 00:02:31.772
Near geographic north, its magnetic
polarity attracts the north-seeking

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:35.662
end of a compass, south-like
polarity in bar magnet language.

00:02:36.395 --> 00:02:39.557
So the geographic and magnetic
labels can be confusing.

00:02:40.105 --> 00:02:44.801
A real compass also has variation
from true north depending on location.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:46.376
Exactly.

00:02:46.641 --> 00:02:50.445
Use the attraction rule and the actual
diagram rather than memorizing a

00:02:50.446 --> 00:02:52.390
confusing label without its meaning.

00:02:52.782 --> 00:02:55.024
A permanent magnet retains magnetism.

00:02:55.246 --> 00:02:58.315
A temporary magnetic material
loses much of it readily.

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:02.903
A domain model represents small regions
whose magnetic directions become

00:03:02.903 --> 00:03:05.045
more aligned during magnetization.

00:03:05.837 --> 00:03:10.608
Soft iron is useful when we want easy
magnetization and demagnetization.

00:03:11.216 --> 00:03:15.289
Permanent magnets use materials that
retain their magnetic state more strongly.

00:03:16.076 --> 00:03:16.899
Exactly.

00:03:17.252 --> 00:03:21.758
Strong heating, mechanical disturbance,
or suitable alternating fields can

00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:26.448
demagnetize some magnets, but those
are explanations, not home experiments.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.318
We will use safe diagrams rather
than hammering or heating anything.

00:03:31.174 --> 00:03:34.016
Current through a wire
produces a magnetic field.

00:03:34.587 --> 00:03:37.116
A coil combines the effects of its turns.

00:03:37.628 --> 00:03:41.308
What makes an electromagnet useful
compared with a permanent magnet?

00:03:42.042 --> 00:03:45.548
We can control it using
current, switch it, change its

00:03:45.553 --> 00:03:48.985
strength, and reverse its poles
by reversing current direction.

00:03:49.801 --> 00:03:50.154
Good.

00:03:50.642 --> 00:03:53.712
Removing current removes the
coil's current-produced field.

00:03:54.038 --> 00:03:58.095
A core may retain some residual
magnetism, so saying every real

00:03:58.095 --> 00:04:02.575
electromagnet becomes perfectly
non-magnetic instantly is too absolute.

00:04:03.318 --> 00:04:07.694
More turns, a larger current, and
a suitable iron core can strengthen

00:04:07.694 --> 00:04:09.784
it with other conditions controlled.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:11.155
Correct.

00:04:11.494 --> 00:04:16.277
More turns can also change resistance,
so control current when testing turns.

00:04:16.784 --> 00:04:18.484
Never just attach a bigger battery.

00:04:18.673 --> 00:04:21.716
Excessive current heats
wires and can damage cells.

00:04:22.108 --> 00:04:26.222
Use teacher-approved current-limited
low-voltage equipment.

00:04:27.012 --> 00:04:31.352
A scrapyard crane can lift magnetic
scrap, a relay can operate another

00:04:31.355 --> 00:04:35.215
switch, and motors and loudspeakers
use electromagnetic effects.

00:04:35.596 --> 00:04:39.988
Not every object on a scrapyard crane
is simply attracted because it is metal
